1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,230
Imagine it's 2045.

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:05,710
The tenth Starship has
just landed on Mars.

3
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:09,930
There are no astronauts inside
planning to go home in a few months.

4
00:00:11,010 --> 00:00:12,940
These people have just moved there.

5
00:00:13,330 --> 00:00:17,600
And they brought construction equipment,
reactors, solar panels, factories...

6
00:00:17,810 --> 00:00:21,780
everything they need to build the
first real city on another planet.

7
00:00:22,810 --> 00:00:23,790
Now here's the question.

8
00:00:23,870 --> 00:00:25,300
Who owns Mars?

9
00:00:26,634 --> 00:00:27,774
Does it belong to Elon Musk?

10
00:00:27,854 --> 00:00:29,724
Or to the United States?

11
00:00:30,554 --> 00:00:31,594
Does SpaceX own it?

12
00:00:31,674 --> 00:00:33,744
Or does nobody own it at all?

13
00:00:34,155 --> 00:00:36,295
And what happens when, fifty years later,

14
00:00:36,375 --> 00:00:38,505
there are millions of
people living on Mars?

15
00:00:39,495 --> 00:00:41,225
Will they still answer to Earth?

16
00:00:41,805 --> 00:00:44,755
Or will they eventually
say: "Thanks for the help.

17
00:00:45,005 --> 00:00:47,135
But from now on, we govern ourselves."

18
00:00:49,945 --> 00:00:53,675
In 1967, the Outer Space
Treaty came into force.

19
00:00:54,725 --> 00:00:57,425
Among other things, it says
that no country can claim the

20
00:00:57,505 --> 00:01:01,215
Moon, Mars, or any other
celestial body as its territory.

21
00:01:02,185 --> 00:01:06,085
So if Elon Musk lands on Mars and
plants a SpaceX flag, he can't

22
00:01:06,165 --> 00:01:10,115
say: "I hereby declare Mars to
be part of the United States."

23
00:01:11,035 --> 00:01:12,525
Mars isn't America.

24
00:01:13,115 --> 00:01:14,985
And SpaceX isn't a country.

25
00:01:15,845 --> 00:01:19,175
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

26
00:01:19,945 --> 00:01:21,415
It really helps the channel.

27
00:01:22,335 --> 00:01:25,185
SpaceX, however, seems to
see it a little differently.

28
00:01:26,295 --> 00:01:30,965
In 2020, it published the terms of service
for Starlink, its satellite internet.

29
00:01:31,215 --> 00:01:34,245
And buried in them is
a sentence about Mars.

30
00:01:35,495 --> 00:01:39,045
Both parties, it says, recognise
Mars as a free planet...

31
00:01:39,855 --> 00:01:41,835
and that no Earth-based
government has authority

32
00:01:41,915 --> 00:01:44,465
or sovereignty over Martian activities.

33
00:01:46,425 --> 00:01:50,255
A satellite internet contract that
already tells Earth to stay out.

34
00:01:51,105 --> 00:01:53,295
But the treaty has an answer to that, too.

35
00:01:54,365 --> 00:01:57,185
It says that private space
activities have to be authorised

36
00:01:57,265 --> 00:02:00,815
and continuously supervised
by the appropriate state.

37
00:02:01,745 --> 00:02:05,725
So if SpaceX is doing something
on Mars, the United States still

38
00:02:05,805 --> 00:02:09,155
has international responsibilities
connected to those activities.

39
00:02:10,495 --> 00:02:13,535
But what exactly does "supervise"
mean when the company is,

40
00:02:13,615 --> 00:02:17,185
most of the time, hundreds of
millions of kilometres away?

41
00:02:18,395 --> 00:02:21,285
There is no lawyer from
Washington standing on Mars.

42
00:02:21,735 --> 00:02:24,065
There is no American police force.

43
00:02:24,815 --> 00:02:27,485
And you definitely can't
just send a sheriff over.

44
00:02:30,035 --> 00:02:32,345
So let's imagine the first few decades.

45
00:02:33,275 --> 00:02:36,585
SpaceX builds the first
base. Then another.

46
00:02:37,395 --> 00:02:40,065
China builds one. Europe builds another.

47
00:02:40,295 --> 00:02:41,685
India builds another.

48
00:02:42,275 --> 00:02:46,085
Maybe international partnerships
and private companies join in too.

49
00:02:46,793 --> 00:02:49,323
And because Mars is really, really big...

50
00:02:49,793 --> 00:02:52,003
nobody is going to
occupy the whole planet.

51
00:02:52,853 --> 00:02:56,103
Instead, there will be individual
islands of civilisation.

52
00:02:56,875 --> 00:03:01,505
One city here. A mining operation
two thousand kilometres away.

53
00:03:02,235 --> 00:03:04,325
A Chinese settlement somewhere else.

54
00:03:05,355 --> 00:03:07,365
And between them... nothing.

55
00:03:08,435 --> 00:03:10,445
Thousands of kilometres
of Martian wilderness.

56
00:03:12,845 --> 00:03:14,255
Here's another problem.

57
00:03:14,585 --> 00:03:19,555
Suppose someone lands on Mars and
builds a city. You own the city.

58
00:03:19,945 --> 00:03:22,135
You own the buildings.
You own the reactor.

59
00:03:22,385 --> 00:03:24,515
You own everything you've built.

60
00:03:25,385 --> 00:03:27,495
But you don't own the
land it's sitting on.

61
00:03:28,915 --> 00:03:31,245
Now imagine you have a settlement on Mars.

62
00:03:31,995 --> 00:03:34,325
There are a hundred thousand
people living there.

63
00:03:34,735 --> 00:03:37,505
You have a nuclear reactor.
You have a water supply.

64
00:03:38,135 --> 00:03:41,635
And one day, another company
arrives and says: "Hi.

65
00:03:41,715 --> 00:03:43,625
We're going to build our base right here."

66
00:03:44,664 --> 00:03:46,844
Right next to you. And you say: "Uh...

67
00:03:47,144 --> 00:03:48,514
we'd rather you didn't."

68
00:03:49,144 --> 00:03:51,674
They say: "Why not?
Nobody owns this land."

69
00:03:52,444 --> 00:03:54,014
And technically, they have a point.

70
00:03:54,504 --> 00:03:58,114
But their landing could throw
enormous amounts of dust into the air.

71
00:03:58,764 --> 00:04:00,894
They could contaminate your water source.

72
00:04:01,584 --> 00:04:02,974
They could damage your equipment.

73
00:04:03,804 --> 00:04:06,574
So we start getting into
the idea of safety zones.

74
00:04:07,384 --> 00:04:09,794
Not: "This territory belongs to us."

75
00:04:10,574 --> 00:04:12,914
But: "Before you operate
here, talk to us first,

76
00:04:12,994 --> 00:04:15,224
because you might interfere
with our activities."

77
00:04:16,744 --> 00:04:21,694
In 2020, the United States and seven other
countries signed the Artemis Accords...

78
00:04:22,384 --> 00:04:25,934
principles for exploring the
Moon, Mars, comets and asteroids.

79
00:04:26,844 --> 00:04:29,214
Safety zones are written right into them.

80
00:04:29,984 --> 00:04:34,374
Legally, that's something very different
from owning the land. Practically...

81
00:04:34,544 --> 00:04:37,354
it starts looking
suspiciously like property.

82
00:04:40,594 --> 00:04:43,854
When European powers colonised
other parts of the world, they

83
00:04:43,934 --> 00:04:47,174
encountered enormous areas that, from
their perspective, were simply...

84
00:04:47,254 --> 00:04:48,944
available.

85
00:04:50,134 --> 00:04:52,584
Never mind that people
already lived there.

86
00:04:53,944 --> 00:04:57,654
Then someone arrived. They built
a settlement. More people arrived.

87
00:04:57,944 --> 00:04:59,144
Governments appeared.

88
00:04:59,394 --> 00:05:02,044
And eventually it became
rather difficult to say:

89
00:05:02,374 --> 00:05:05,144
"It's just a few people
living somewhere far away."

90
00:05:05,774 --> 00:05:07,484
Because it had become a society.

91
00:05:10,154 --> 00:05:11,584
Take the American colonies.

92
00:05:12,514 --> 00:05:14,644
Britain could say: "You are our subjects."

93
00:05:15,334 --> 00:05:16,844
And legally, that made sense.

94
00:05:17,794 --> 00:05:21,854
But eventually the colonies had their
own cities, their own economy, their

95
00:05:21,934 --> 00:05:25,784
own political institutions, their own
military, and their own interests.

96
00:05:26,794 --> 00:05:29,454
And eventually came the
question: why should we be

97
00:05:29,534 --> 00:05:32,484
governed by a government
thousands of kilometres away?

98
00:05:35,134 --> 00:05:39,084
Now put that on Mars.
Imagine the year 2150.

99
00:05:39,474 --> 00:05:41,924
There are ten million
people living on Mars.

100
00:05:42,854 --> 00:05:44,034
Most of them were born there.

101
00:05:44,114 --> 00:05:45,464
They have never been to Earth.

102
00:05:46,174 --> 00:05:48,064
Earth is something they know from history.

103
00:05:48,934 --> 00:05:50,964
Like we know the Roman Empire today.

104
00:05:51,694 --> 00:05:53,864
And then some government
on Earth tells them:

105
00:05:54,074 --> 00:05:55,984
"Under our laws, you
are still our citizens."

106
00:05:57,094 --> 00:06:00,104
The Martian government
replies: "No, we're not."

107
00:06:00,824 --> 00:06:03,494
Earth insists: "You
have to obey our laws."

108
00:06:04,104 --> 00:06:06,274
Mars asks: "And why would we do that?"

109
00:06:06,704 --> 00:06:08,174
"Because we founded you."

110
00:06:08,664 --> 00:06:10,314
"That was a hundred years ago."

111
00:06:12,814 --> 00:06:16,104
Now we reach a problem that
no law can completely solve.

112
00:06:16,954 --> 00:06:18,984
A state isn't just a legal document.

113
00:06:19,354 --> 00:06:22,744
It's also the ability to
actually exercise power.

114
00:06:23,604 --> 00:06:26,254
If Washington gives an order
to a Martian settlement...

115
00:06:26,444 --> 00:06:30,814
how exactly does it enforce
it? Do you send the police?

116
00:06:31,384 --> 00:06:33,794
You have to transport
them to another planet.

117
00:06:34,874 --> 00:06:37,404
Do you send the military? Sure.

118
00:06:38,134 --> 00:06:39,994
But an interplanetary war is slightly more

119
00:06:40,074 --> 00:06:42,784
complicated than sending
tanks across a border.

120
00:06:43,704 --> 00:06:48,294
And if Mars already has its own industry,
energy infrastructure and defences...

121
00:06:48,624 --> 00:06:53,354
then the situation starts looking a
lot like any other independent power.

122
00:06:56,124 --> 00:06:58,114
Could Mars itself become a country?

123
00:06:58,744 --> 00:07:00,414
And here's the surprising part.

124
00:07:00,684 --> 00:07:04,114
Current space law doesn't
really provide a clear answer.

125
00:07:05,274 --> 00:07:09,584
The Outer Space Treaty says: "You
cannot claim Mars as your territory."

126
00:07:10,514 --> 00:07:13,944
But it doesn't provide a particularly
elegant answer to this question:

127
00:07:14,365 --> 00:07:17,625
what happens if, after
several generations, a society

128
00:07:17,705 --> 00:07:21,335
develops on Mars and decides
to create its own state?

129
00:07:24,245 --> 00:07:27,095
Two science-fiction shows
illustrate this beautifully.

130
00:07:31,585 --> 00:07:33,035
First, For All Mankind.

131
00:07:34,125 --> 00:07:38,775
Mars is still primarily a place where
Earth powers compete. The United States.

132
00:07:38,865 --> 00:07:41,675
The Soviet Union. Later, other players.

133
00:07:42,648 --> 00:07:47,918
Everyone wants a base. Everyone wants
resources. Everyone wants to be first.

134
00:07:48,828 --> 00:07:52,058
Mars is essentially an extension
of Earth's geopolitics.

135
00:07:52,888 --> 00:07:54,318
Then there's The Expanse.

136
00:07:55,168 --> 00:07:57,678
And there, the situation
is completely different.

137
00:07:58,508 --> 00:08:00,608
Mars isn't just a colony any more.

138
00:08:00,688 --> 00:08:03,058
It's a civilisation of its own.

139
00:08:03,998 --> 00:08:07,818
It has its own government. Its
own military. Its own economy.

140
00:08:07,898 --> 00:08:10,568
Its own culture. And its own interests.

141
00:08:11,942 --> 00:08:17,012
And that may be what eventually happens
in reality. Not in twenty years.

142
00:08:17,602 --> 00:08:19,464
Probably not in a hundred.

143
00:08:19,614 --> 00:08:22,324
But if people stay on Mars
for several generations...

144
00:08:22,734 --> 00:08:25,334
it's hard to imagine that
it would remain nothing

145
00:08:25,414 --> 00:08:27,844
more than a distant
colony of Earth forever.

146
00:08:30,784 --> 00:08:34,354
Let's go back to Elon. Because
that's where we started.

147
00:08:35,144 --> 00:08:38,934
Imagine SpaceX really does
establish the first Martian colony.

148
00:08:39,664 --> 00:08:42,874
The first Martian city might
even be named Musk City.

149
00:08:43,664 --> 00:08:45,594
But a hundred and fifty years later?

150
00:08:46,164 --> 00:08:48,054
Millions of people are living there.

151
00:08:48,784 --> 00:08:50,854
Elon Musk has been dead for generations.

152
00:08:51,904 --> 00:08:54,764
And some teacher is telling
students: "The first Martian

153
00:08:54,844 --> 00:08:58,174
settlement was established
by a company called SpaceX."

154
00:08:58,544 --> 00:09:02,414
And some student asks:
"Wait, what was SpaceX?"

155
00:09:04,814 --> 00:09:06,144
So here's the paradox.

156
00:09:07,154 --> 00:09:12,664
The Outer Space Treaty may prevent Elon
Musk from saying in 2045: "Mars is mine."

157
00:09:13,554 --> 00:09:18,144
But it may not prevent Mars from
saying, in 2200: "Mars is ours."

158
00:09:18,974 --> 00:09:21,604
And I think that's the much
more interesting question.

159
00:09:24,844 --> 00:09:26,584
The first stage of colonisation will

160
00:09:26,664 --> 00:09:29,274
probably look something
like For All Mankind.

161
00:09:30,104 --> 00:09:34,554
Countries and corporations will compete
for bases, resources and prestige.

162
00:09:35,734 --> 00:09:38,184
But if colonisation actually succeeds...

163
00:09:38,294 --> 00:09:42,504
we might eventually end up in
something much closer to The Expanse.

164
00:09:43,224 --> 00:09:46,154
And then the important question
won't be "Who owns Mars?"

165
00:09:46,844 --> 00:09:50,974
It will be: who gets to decide how the
people living on Mars are governed?

166
00:09:53,534 --> 00:09:56,144
Mars may never belong to a single country.

167
00:09:56,794 --> 00:09:58,424
It may never belong to SpaceX.

168
00:09:59,554 --> 00:10:02,164
It may not even belong
to humanity as a whole.

169
00:10:03,144 --> 00:10:07,514
Maybe one day it will have new countries,
new nations, and new political identities.

170
00:10:09,694 --> 00:10:12,354
And hundreds of years from
now, someone on Mars might look

171
00:10:12,434 --> 00:10:16,164
at Earth and ask: "So our
ancestors really came from there?"

172
00:10:16,714 --> 00:10:19,514
And Earth will be to them what
Europe is to many people in

173
00:10:19,594 --> 00:10:23,644
the Americas today: the place
their ancestors once came from.

174
00:10:25,134 --> 00:10:28,184
Perhaps that is where the real
history of Mars will begin.

175
00:10:28,414 --> 00:10:30,964
Not when the first Starship lands.

176
00:10:31,414 --> 00:10:33,874
But when the first Martians look
at Earth and say the sentence

177
00:10:33,954 --> 00:10:38,064
humanity has heard many times
before: "We are not your colony."
